1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,040
On the morning after Pearl Harbor,

2
00:00:02,330 --> 00:00:05,220
a young Japanese naval officer woke up on a beach

3
00:00:05,070 --> 00:00:08,419
in Hawaii and found an American rifle pointed at his

4
00:00:08,269 --> 00:00:11,780
face. His name was Kazuo Sakamaki.

5
00:00:12,689 --> 00:00:15,300
The day before, he had tried to steer a midget

6
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:16,619
submarine into the harbor.

7
00:00:17,510 --> 00:00:19,320
The submarine had failed.

8
00:00:19,829 --> 00:00:22,399
So had the charge he set to destroy it.

9
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:25,359
He had jumped into the sea expecting to die,

10
00:00:25,210 --> 00:00:27,099
and the sea had thrown him back.

11
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:30,589
He asked his captors for one thing.

12
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:33,229
He asked to be allowed to kill himself.

13
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:34,909
They refused.

14
00:00:36,225 --> 00:00:39,115
Ten young men had gone into Pearl Harbor in those

15
00:00:38,965 --> 00:00:43,534
five midget submarines. Japan would soon celebrate nine of them

16
00:00:43,384 --> 00:00:45,814
as heroes who had died for the Emperor.

17
00:00:46,765 --> 00:00:49,094
There was no place on that list for the tenth.

18
00:00:50,674 --> 00:00:53,684
Sakamaki had been taught a simple rule.

19
00:00:54,255 --> 00:00:58,064
The Japanese military field code told every soldier that rather

20
00:00:57,914 --> 00:01:00,684
than live and bear the shame of imprisonment by the

21
00:01:00,534 --> 00:01:02,004
enemy, he should die.

22
00:01:02,914 --> 00:01:06,045
A soldier was expected to save his last bullet for

23
00:01:05,894 --> 00:01:06,405
himself.

24
00:01:07,625 --> 00:01:10,875
The rule worked. In the Burma campaign,

25
00:01:11,165 --> 00:01:16,455
Allied forces counted seventeen thousand four hundred and sixty-six Japanese

26
00:01:16,305 --> 00:01:18,555
dead and only one hundred and forty-two prisoners.

27
00:01:19,525 --> 00:01:20,635
Across the whole war,

28
00:01:20,905 --> 00:01:24,255
the United States held about five thousand four hundred Japanese

29
00:01:24,105 --> 00:01:26,075
prisoners on its own soil.

30
00:01:26,944 --> 00:01:29,255
So what do you do with men who believe they

31
00:01:29,105 --> 00:01:30,155
should already be dead?

32
00:01:30,924 --> 00:01:33,915
In Wisconsin, the answer would turn out to include a

33
00:01:33,765 --> 00:01:34,695
baseball diamond.

34
00:01:36,275 --> 00:01:39,344
Sakamaki spent his first weeks as a prisoner in a

35
00:01:39,194 --> 00:01:41,145
wire enclosure on Sand Island,

36
00:01:40,995 --> 00:01:45,125
in Honolulu harbor. He later called it a birdcage,

37
00:01:44,974 --> 00:01:47,585
about ten feet by ten.

38
00:01:47,435 --> 00:01:49,785
He thought constantly about suicide.

39
00:01:50,974 --> 00:01:54,105
On February twenty-ninth, nineteen forty-two,

40
00:01:53,954 --> 00:01:55,165
he was shipped to the mainland,

41
00:01:55,415 --> 00:01:58,385
held briefly at Angel Island in San Francisco Bay,

42
00:01:58,594 --> 00:01:59,605
and put on a train.

43
00:02:00,635 --> 00:02:02,585
Nobody told him where he was going.

44
00:02:03,415 --> 00:02:04,945
When he finally stepped down,

45
00:02:04,795 --> 00:02:07,405
in March, the air was cold and unfamiliar,

46
00:02:07,734 --> 00:02:09,305
and he had to ask where he was.

47
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:11,789
He was at Camp McCoy,

48
00:02:11,639 --> 00:02:13,670
near the small town of Sparta,

49
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:16,430
Wisconsin. For the next six months,

50
00:02:16,539 --> 00:02:19,990
while German and Italian prisoners poured into Allied camps by

51
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:24,370
the thousands, Sakamaki was the only Japanese military prisoner in

52
00:02:24,219 --> 00:02:28,230
American hands. In July nineteen forty-two,

53
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:32,330
nine more arrived. By the summer of nineteen forty-five,

54
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:35,125
there would be more than two thousand seven hundred.

55
00:02:36,074 --> 00:02:39,925
Japanese soldiers had been told that capture meant torture and

56
00:02:39,775 --> 00:02:44,125
humiliation. What they found at McCoy was harder to explain.

57
00:02:45,275 --> 00:02:47,564
Steamed rice was served at least once a day.

58
00:02:48,414 --> 00:02:50,545
There was soy sauce with every meal.

59
00:02:51,435 --> 00:02:53,385
The Red Cross supplied chopsticks.

60
00:02:54,335 --> 00:02:57,705
The prisoners were allowed to plant their own vegetable gardens.

61
00:02:58,949 --> 00:03:01,500
None of this made the shame go away.

62
00:03:02,409 --> 00:03:05,900
Sakamaki later wrote that their desire for suicide was thwarted

63
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:09,819
on every hand. There were no knives and no ropes.

64
00:03:10,750 --> 00:03:13,140
Some men banged their heads against walls.

65
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:15,480
Some refused to eat.

66
00:03:16,229 --> 00:03:17,280
We wanted to die,

67
00:03:17,289 --> 00:03:19,120
he wrote, and yet we could not die.

68
00:03:22,150 --> 00:03:23,900
The Americans discovered that the shame could also be used.

69
00:03:24,889 --> 00:03:27,000
Prisoners were allowed to write home every week,

70
00:03:27,150 --> 00:03:29,260
but the Japanese almost never did.

71
00:03:30,169 --> 00:03:32,520
A letter would tell their families they were alive,

72
00:03:32,689 --> 00:03:34,360
which meant they had been captured.

73
00:03:35,250 --> 00:03:37,379
So when a Japanese prisoner caused trouble,

74
00:03:37,610 --> 00:03:39,200
the guards had a quiet threat.

75
00:03:40,009 --> 00:03:42,099
They could write to his family themselves.

76
00:03:43,030 --> 00:03:44,960
It usually ended the trouble.

77
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:49,770
The camp commander was Lieutenant Colonel Horace Rogers.

78
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:53,189
His approach was to keep the men busy.

79
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:57,750
At first, many Japanese prisoners refused to take part in

80
00:03:57,599 --> 00:04:00,230
anything. Then boredom won.

81
00:04:01,375 --> 00:04:03,745
They built a stage in each compound.

82
00:04:04,634 --> 00:04:07,205
They held concerts, poetry contests and,

83
00:04:07,055 --> 00:04:10,724
above all, plays. Up to three productions a month,

84
00:04:10,574 --> 00:04:13,205
with costumes and scenery made by hand.

85
00:04:14,215 --> 00:04:15,745
Rogers had one condition.

86
00:04:16,555 --> 00:04:18,264
He wanted to be allowed to watch,

87
00:04:18,115 --> 00:04:21,004
and he came to as many performances as he could.

88
00:04:21,875 --> 00:04:23,185
Without tools for wood,

89
00:04:23,454 --> 00:04:26,444
the prisoners made toys out of cigarette paper glued together

90
00:04:26,295 --> 00:04:27,484
with rice water.

91
00:04:28,649 --> 00:04:31,879
Then the YMCA donated baseball equipment.

92
00:04:32,850 --> 00:04:36,000
Baseball became the prisoners' favourite outdoor activity.

93
00:04:36,949 --> 00:04:39,199
Teams formed in the two Japanese compounds,

94
00:04:39,410 --> 00:04:40,639
played a regular season,

95
00:04:40,889 --> 00:04:43,099
and met in a camp championship series.

96
00:04:44,129 --> 00:04:48,000
When inspectors toured McCoy in September nineteen forty-five,

97
00:04:47,850 --> 00:04:51,019
they reported more athletic equipment than at any other camp

98
00:04:50,870 --> 00:04:51,699
they had seen.

99
00:04:52,850 --> 00:04:56,519
On Saturdays, the prisoners were marched outside the wire to

100
00:04:56,370 --> 00:05:00,979
watch American films. The War Department had expected the Japanese

101
00:05:00,829 --> 00:05:03,139
to seize any chance to break out.

102
00:05:03,930 --> 00:05:05,000
On the way to the movies,

103
00:05:05,209 --> 00:05:06,060
they never tried.

104
00:05:07,649 --> 00:05:09,939
McCoy was not a holiday camp.

105
00:05:10,829 --> 00:05:11,939
There were double fences,

106
00:05:12,350 --> 00:05:13,859
guard towers with machine guns,

107
00:05:13,709 --> 00:05:15,939
and military police with war dogs.

108
00:05:16,949 --> 00:05:19,239
The Japanese prisoners complained constantly,

109
00:05:19,529 --> 00:05:22,739
and their spokesman was none other than Kazuo Sakamaki.

110
00:05:23,470 --> 00:05:26,339
They did not want to wash laundry where American women

111
00:05:26,189 --> 00:05:29,719
worked. They wanted coal instead of wood.

112
00:05:30,610 --> 00:05:32,719
On one morning in May nineteen forty-four,

113
00:05:32,569 --> 00:05:36,479
the enlisted men staged a sit-down strike on their officers'

114
00:05:36,509 --> 00:05:41,120
orders. Rogers sent in military police with fixed bayonets.

115
00:05:41,970 --> 00:05:43,299
The men went to work.

116
00:05:44,399 --> 00:05:45,829
And some still ran.

117
00:05:46,639 --> 00:05:47,870
Across the whole country,

118
00:05:48,139 --> 00:05:51,089
Japanese prisoners made fourteen escape attempts.

119
00:05:52,019 --> 00:05:54,029
Every one of them was from Camp McCoy,

120
00:05:53,879 --> 00:05:55,489
and not one succeeded.

121
00:05:56,379 --> 00:05:58,289
In May nineteen forty-five,

122
00:05:58,139 --> 00:06:01,089
three young prisoners dug under a fence with a stolen

123
00:06:00,939 --> 00:06:04,909
Texaco road map. A farmer spotted one of them a

124
00:06:04,759 --> 00:06:08,209
week later, poling down the Mississippi River on a homemade

125
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:11,750
raft. The other two were caught walking along a highway,

126
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:15,769
still wearing uniforms with the letters P W stencilled in

127
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:17,469
bright yellow on the front and back.

128
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:22,329
In July, another escapee knocked at a farmhouse door and

129
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:24,189
rubbed his stomach to show he was hungry.

130
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:27,750
The farm wife gave him bread and ran to the

131
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,709
telephone. When the highway patrol arrived,

132
00:06:30,980 --> 00:06:33,289
he was still waiting politely at the back door for

133
00:06:33,139 --> 00:06:33,949
the next course.

134
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:38,529
The last Japanese prisoner to escape was Yuzo Ohashi,

135
00:06:38,839 --> 00:06:40,269
captured on Iwo Jima.

136
00:06:41,019 --> 00:06:44,509
He believed Mexico was about three hundred miles south of

137
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:47,069
the camp. He walked for four days.

138
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:49,949
He was caught on September second,

139
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:53,189
nineteen forty-five, the day Japan formally surrendered.

140
00:06:54,079 --> 00:06:56,870
He told his guards that Japan had clearly lost,

141
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:58,889
and that he could not go home in shame.

142
00:06:59,699 --> 00:07:01,209
It was escape, he said,

143
00:07:01,259 --> 00:07:01,909
or suicide.

144
00:07:03,550 --> 00:07:06,539
That was exactly what American officials feared.

145
00:07:07,410 --> 00:07:08,519
As the war ended,

146
00:07:08,790 --> 00:07:11,959
they expected mass suicides among the Japanese prisoners.

147
00:07:13,050 --> 00:07:16,959
A Japanese language newspaper in Denver even predicted one at

148
00:07:16,810 --> 00:07:17,519
Camp McCoy.

149
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:22,729
It never happened. Before the prisoners were sent home,

150
00:07:23,259 --> 00:07:26,509
Japanese American counselors spoke to them about the value of

151
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:30,589
their lives. They told them that Japan itself had surrendered,

152
00:07:30,439 --> 00:07:33,250
and that no man needed to die to restore his

153
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:36,989
family's honor. There were no suicides on the ships back

154
00:07:36,839 --> 00:07:37,529
to Japan.

155
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:43,929
Kazuo Sakamaki went home in nineteen forty-six.

156
00:07:43,779 --> 00:07:46,429
Japan had treated him as if he had never existed.

157
00:07:47,279 --> 00:07:49,149
He became a lifelong pacifist,

158
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:53,229
joined Toyota, and in nineteen sixty-nine became president of its

159
00:07:53,079 --> 00:07:57,789
Brazilian subsidiary. For decades he refused to talk about the

160
00:07:57,639 --> 00:08:01,469
war. He finally spoke in public in nineteen ninety-one,

161
00:08:01,319 --> 00:08:03,469
at a conference in Texas,

162
00:08:03,740 --> 00:08:06,669
half a century after he had asked his captors for

163
00:08:06,519 --> 00:08:07,449
permission to die.

164
00:08:08,524 --> 00:08:11,495
In nineteen sixty-six, another former prisoner,

165
00:08:11,345 --> 00:08:15,674
Yasuichiro Hayashi, asked through the Japanese embassy whether he might

166
00:08:15,524 --> 00:08:17,014
visit Camp McCoy again.

167
00:08:17,865 --> 00:08:18,814
For him, he wrote,

168
00:08:19,064 --> 00:08:21,995
that place signified a second starting point in his life.

169
00:08:23,125 --> 00:08:25,995
If you know another prisoner account we should look into,

170
00:08:26,245 --> 00:08:27,214
put it in the comments.

171
00:08:28,824 --> 00:08:31,674
The field code had told these men that a prisoner

172
00:08:31,524 --> 00:08:34,715
was already dead. In the middle of Wisconsin,

173
00:08:35,065 --> 00:08:36,674
some of them found out otherwise.

174
00:08:37,664 --> 00:08:39,715
Someone handed them a bat and a glove,

175
00:08:39,565 --> 00:08:41,654
and told them it was their turn.
